<!--
  jt-account-guard: account security across services
  File:    zzzz-132700-jt_account_guard_rules.xml
  Range:   132700-132799
  Licence: AGPL-3.0-or-later    Jason Tools Co., Ltd.

  Every other jt- pack sees one product. An address that sprays the single
  sign-on, the mail server and the file cloud, or that logs in on one service
  minutes after it was failing on another, is invisible to each of them. These
  rules correlate the login events of all packs through three groups that the
  packs put on their own login rules (the convention, see the manifest notes):

    jt_auth_success   one event per successful login of a named account
    jt_auth_failure   one event per failed login (wrong password, unknown,
                      disabled or locked account); one copy per attempt
    jt_auth_lockout   the product locked or throttled an account or address

  with srcip = the client address (plain, no ::ffff:) and dstuser = the
  account as the product writes it.

  ★ Why the file is named zzzz-.
    if_group attaches a rule under the rules that carry the group and are
    already loaded, and if_matched_group only counts rules that are loaded
    when it is read (OS_MarkGroup, rules_list.c; a later rule is never
    counted and analysisd -t says nothing). Rule files load in file name
    order across the built-in and the custom directory: zzzz- sorts after
    every pack (zz-...) and after a site file named zzz-.

  ★ How the rules see other services.
    Correlation is per agent unless the rule says global_frequency
    (eventinfo.c). Two services are two different locations: an agent's
    location is "(agent name) address->log file", so different_location
    means another host or another log file. One product logging to two files
    on one host would look like two services; the packs tag one copy per
    attempt so that it does not.

  ★ What the rules can and cannot count.
    An event enters an if_matched_group list only when the rule it ended on
    is not the target of an if_matched_sid anywhere (analysisd.c: the sid
    list wins, the group lists are skipped). A pack that counts its own
    failures with if_matched_sid on the failure rule therefore shows its
    failures here as the CURRENT event (the rules below are children of
    every tagged rule) but never as an EARLIER one. Packs that count with a
    pack-only group instead are fully visible: Keycloak, Samba AD, the UCS
    management console, OPNsense (web interface, services, OpenVPN),
    Proxmox VE (pveproxy), PMG, the jt-ipam audit line, Zimbra and Z-Push.
    Still if_matched_sid based, so current-event only: Nextcloud 131710,
    PBS 131802, Synology 132020, LibreNMS 131942, Odoo 132315, AdGuard
    130931 (their failure rules have several parent paths, see below).
    A rule with several parents is stored once per parent path (OS_MarkGroup
    walks every tree node): one jt-ipam audit failure counts twice, one
    Zimbra brute-force alert five times. An earlier event that already
    raised a correlation at this rule's level or above (a pack's level 10
    brute-force alert) ends the search instead of being counted.

  ★ Levels.
    A correlation rule only fires when its level is above the level an
    earlier event already reached through another correlation
    (Search_LastGroups: lf->matched >= rule->level ends the search), hence
    13 after the level 12 spraying rule. Children replace their parent's
    alert, so every rule here is above the highest tagged failure (8) and
    success (10) rule it can hang under.

  ★ Addresses.
    "Public" is: not in 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 127/8, 100.64/10,
    fc00::/7 or fe80::/10, one negated srcip per range (several positive
    srcip elements would be OR; a negated ::1 in any spelling also matches
    every IPv4 address, so IPv6 loopback is not excluded here; the packs
    drop or re-level it in their own decoders and rules).
    Address-based rules only use public sources: inside the network the
    same address belongs to a reverse proxy, a NAT gateway, a vulnerability
    scanner or a server that checks passwords for others (an LDAP bind is
    the echo of somebody else's login), and the per-pack rules already
    count failures by internal address.

  Ideas re-implemented in our own words, not copied (Sigma is DRL 1.1,
  Elastic detection rules are Elastic License 2.0):
    MITRE ATT&CK T1110.003 Password Spraying, T1110.004 Credential
      Stuffing, T1110.001 Password Guessing, T1078 Valid Accounts
      https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/003/
      https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/004/
    Sigma correlations, value_count: failed logons for many distinct
      accounts from one address within a time span (password spraying)
      https://sigmahq.io/docs/meta/correlations.html
    Elastic, "Multiple Logon Failure Followed by Logon Success" (same
      source, failures then success) and "Potential Okta Password Spray
      (Single Source)"
      https://github.com/elastic/detection-rules/blob/main/rules/windows/credential_access_bruteforce_multiple_logon_failure_followed_by_success.toml
    CISA AA24-290A (2024): password spraying and MFA push bombing against
      many services of one organisation, then logins with the found password
      https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa24-290a
-->

<group name="jt_account_guard,">

  <!-- ===== Cross-service password spraying from one public address =====
    The current failure (any tagged pack) plus at least two earlier failures
    from the same address, each on another service or host than the current
    one and each for another account name than the current one, within ten
    minutes. different_user leaves out the copies of one login that two
    products write (an ActiveSync login is logged by Z-Push and by Zimbra
    with the same account), and a person retrying one account on two
    services. Per-pack brute-force rules count one service; this one sees an
    address that stays under each of them. -->
  <rule id="132701" level="10" frequency="3" timeframe="600">
    <if_group>jt_auth_failure</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>jt_auth_failure</if_matched_group>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <same_source_ip />
    <different_location />
    <different_user />
    <global_frequency />
    <description>[WARN] jt-account-guard: $(srcip) failed to log in on more than one service within 10 minutes, with several account names (cross-service password spraying); last account $(dstuser)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,jt_account_guard_spray,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="132702" level="12" frequency="6" timeframe="600">
    <if_group>jt_auth_failure</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>jt_auth_failure</if_matched_group>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <same_source_ip />
    <different_location />
    <different_user />
    <global_frequency />
    <description>[HIGH] jt-account-guard: $(srcip) failed to log in six times within 10 minutes on more than one service, with several account names (cross-service password spraying); last account $(dstuser)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,jt_account_guard_spray,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Distributed guessing of one account across services =====
    The current failure for an account, from a public address, plus at least
    four earlier failures for the same account name on other services or
    hosts, each from another address than the current one, within 15
    minutes. Same account name only: products that write the name in another
    form (alice, alice@example.com, EXAMPLE\alice) are not joined. A phone
    with an old password keeps failing from one address; it reaches this
    rule only when the account also fails somewhere else from another
    address within the window. A placeholder instead of a name (one or two dashes,
    written by web access logs and by Nextcloud for a failed login) is not
    an account and is left out. -->
  <rule id="132703" level="10" frequency="5" timeframe="900">
    <if_group>jt_auth_failure</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>jt_auth_failure</if_matched_group>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <user type="pcre2">[[:alnum:]]</user>
    <same_user />
    <different_srcip />
    <different_location />
    <global_frequency />
    <description>[WARN] jt-account-guard: $(dstuser) failed to log in on more than one service from several addresses within 15 minutes (distributed password guessing); last from $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
      <id>T1110.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,jt_account_guard_guess,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== A successful login from an address that was spraying =====
    Any tagged successful login from an address that 132701 or 132702
    reported within the last 30 minutes, on any service. Level 13: above the
    level 12 of 132702, which would otherwise end the search. One alert per
    10 minutes (ignore), since a phone or a sync client repeats its login
    every few seconds. -->
  <rule id="132704" level="13" timeframe="1800" ignore="600">
    <if_group>jt_auth_success</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>jt_account_guard_spray</if_matched_group>
    <same_source_ip />
    <global_frequency />
    <description>[HIGH] jt-account-guard: $(dstuser) logged in from $(srcip), an address that was failing logins on several services with several account names (password spraying that found a password)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_account_guard_success_after_spray,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===== Lockouts, then a login somewhere else =====
    132705: an account locked or throttled on one service (keyed by account
    name), then a successful login of the same account name on another
    service or host, from a public address other than the one the lockout
    named. 132706: an address locked out by one service, then a successful
    login from that address on another service. Both within 15 minutes and
    at most one alert per 10 minutes each. -->
  <rule id="132705" level="12" timeframe="900" ignore="600">
    <if_group>jt_auth_success</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>jt_auth_lockout</if_matched_group>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <same_user />
    <different_srcip />
    <different_location />
    <global_frequency />
    <description>[HIGH] jt-account-guard: $(dstuser) logged in from the public address $(srcip) shortly after another service locked the account out after failed logins from elsewhere</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_account_guard_lockout,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="132706" level="12" timeframe="900" ignore="600">
    <if_group>jt_auth_success</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>jt_auth_lockout</if_matched_group>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <same_source_ip />
    <different_location />
    <global_frequency />
    <description>[HIGH] jt-account-guard: $(dstuser) logged in from $(srcip), an address that another service had just locked out after repeated failed logins</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>jt_account_guard_lockout,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
